    <text><list listType="ordered"><item>Chronic kidney disease G3a A2 (N18.31). Confirmed: eGFR 51 to 58 for more than three months, with albuminuria of 240 and 262 mg/g on two samples. Long-standing untreated hypertension, borderline left ventricular hypertrophy on ECG, a bland urine without red cells, and normal-sized, mildly echogenic kidneys on ultrasound make hypertensive kidney disease the probable cause, possibly worsened by regular naproxen. With no hematuria, systemic features, nephrotic-range albuminuria, or rapid decline, a biopsy or glomerular serology is not needed now. KFRE is low, at 1.6 percent over 5 years. [S]</item><item>Hypertension with chronic kidney disease (I12.9). Above target on losartan 50 mg. Today&apos;s potassium of 4.6 mmol/L and creatinine of 1.26 mg/dL after three weeks allow the dose to be raised. [S]</item><item>Vitamin D deficiency with a mildly raised PTH (E55.9). A PTH of 74 pg/mL at G3a with a low vitamin D is most likely from the vitamin D deficiency; calcium and phosphate are normal. [S]</item></list><paragraph>Data reviewed: The intake results, the ECG, today&apos;s metabolic panel and second urine albumin-creatinine ratio, home blood pressure readings, and this morning&apos;s kidney ultrasound. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
