    <code code="18776-5" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Plan of care note"/>
    <title>Plan of Treatment</title>
    <text><list><item>Raise losartan to 100 mg daily, the maximum dose, for A2 albuminuria. [S]</item><item>Start dapagliflozin 10 mg daily: KDIGO recommends an SGLT2 inhibitor in chronic kidney disease with eGFR of at least 20 and a urine albumin-creatinine ratio of at least 200 mg/g, with or without diabetes. [S]</item><item>Recheck potassium and creatinine 2 to 4 weeks after these changes; accept a creatinine rise of up to 30 percent. [S]</item><item>Aim for a standardized systolic pressure below 120 mmHg if tolerated; if home readings stay above target on the full ARB dose, add amlodipine. [S]</item><item>Repeat the urine albumin-creatinine ratio about three months after starting treatment to judge the response, then eGFR and urine albumin twice a year. [S]</item><item>Continue vitamin D3 1,000 units daily and recheck 25-hydroxyvitamin D, calcium, phosphate, and PTH in 6 to 12 months; no calcitriol. [S]</item><item>No NSAIDs; written sick-day rules for losartan and dapagliflozin during vomiting, diarrhea, or poor intake. [S]</item><item>Complete hepatitis B vaccination, as for all adults with chronic kidney disease. [S]</item><item>Discussed with Dr. Hartwell the same morning. Ms. Dickinson agrees with the plan and asked for it in writing. [S]</item><item>Follow-up: Primary care leads, with home and video visits. Nephrology video review in about a year with the yearly tests, or sooner if eGFR falls by more than 20 percent and stays down, the urine albumin-creatinine ratio doubles, potassium is above 5.5 mmol/L, or the 5-year KFRE reaches 3 percent. [S]</item></list></text>

